      }},
      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 Asian Oceanian Association for the Study of Obesity . Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "In a mice study, insulin suppressed apolipoprotein A-V (apoA-V) gene expression in a dose dependent manner. Thus, we investigated the association between apoA-V levels and dyslipidemias in obese children with hyperinsulinemia."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The subjects were 17 obese children (15 male, 2 female) aged 11.8 ± 2.4 years. Total cholesterol (TC), high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDLC), triglyceride (TG), apoA-V and insulin levels were determined."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Obese children with hyperinsulinemia had greater percent overweight, higher TG level, lower HDLC level and lower apoA-V level than those without hyperinsulinemia. In simple regression analysis, apoA-V level correlated negatively with TG (r = -0.613, p = 0.0152) and insulin levels (r = -0.566, p = 0.0279), and positively correlated with HDLC (r = 0.811, p = 0.0002). In stepwise regression analysis, insulin level emerged as the independent determinant of TG level after apoA-V level was taken into account, whereas apoA-V emerged as the independent determinant of HDLC level after adjusting for insulin level."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Insulin may be a potent regulator of serum apoA-V level in obesity, and apoA-V level may partly contribute to the development of obesity-associated dyslipidemia."
          }
        ]
      },
